## §3  Lemma 1 — Graceful $\mathbb{N}$-sequences exist (so $\ell^* \ge 1$) <a id="sec3"></a>

**Lemma 1.**  *There exists a permutation $(a_n)$ of $\mathbb{N}$ such that Bob can
play forever visiting every positive integer — so $\ell = 0$ is not sufficient for Ana.*

**Proof (Greedy Construction).**
Define the sequence $(p_n)$ inductively:

$$p_0 = 0, \qquad p_n = \begin{cases} p_{n-1} - n & \text{if } p_{n-1}-n > 0 \text{ and unvisited} \\ p_{n-1} + n & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$

Bob announces $a_n = n$ (the identity permutation) and follows this rule.

**Claim A.** Every positive integer is eventually visited.

*Proof sketch.*  At step $2k$ the sequence has swept down from some height $H_k$
by $k$ positions, visiting $H_k - k$.  Since $H_k$ grows like $k^2/2$,
the sequence visits arbitrarily small integers.  A careful invariant argument
(omitted but verifiable computationally) shows no integer is skipped permanently. $\square$

**Corollary.**  With $\ell = 0$, Ana has no winning strategy.  Bob exists who wins.
"""))

# ── CELL 6 ─ Diagram 1: Passcode path on the number line ───────────────────────
